<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imperial Academy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">124 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Imperial Academy, or Taixue, was an institution established under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a> in 124 BCE to train officials in the Confucian classics. It helped make Confucian learning the basis of government service. It was an important foundation of the Han Dynasty's bureaucratic state.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Taixue, translated as the Imperial Academy, Imperial School, Imperial University or Central University, was the highest rank of educational establishment in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and was created during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. It taught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> and Chinese literature, among other subjects, for high civil service posts. During the 2nd century the university held 30,000 students and academicians, and it supplied the Han dynasty with well-educated bureaucrats for the imperial government. The first nationwide government school system in China was established in 3 CE under Emperor Ping of Han, with the Taixue in the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> and local schools in prefectures and in the main cities of smaller counties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a> made major reforms, giving the academy a greater administrative role and renaming it the Guozijian. Successive dynasties maintained it until it was abolished in 1905, near the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>. A civil service based on competitive examination, not recommendation, was introduced only under the Sui and did not become mature until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Northern Song</a> the Kaifeng Taixue was created as part of the Qingli Reforms to educate the children of commoners and low-ranking officials, and it was the only institution that survived the reversal of the reforms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-anshi">Wang Anshi</a>'s New Policies expanded it from 200 students in 1051 to 2,400 in 1079 and organised it into three halls, Outer, Inner and Upper, through which students advanced to become qualified as officials. In 1103 it had 3,800 students. After the fall of the Northern Song the university was refounded in 1142 at Lin'an, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a>, with a quota of 300 students, which grew to 1,000 by 1148.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a> the students of the Imperial University, sometimes joined by students of other capital schools, became one of the most visible and influential political groups. They often protested in the streets over domestic and foreign policy, and some protests led to the dismissal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prefect">prefects</a> and even chief councillors. Attendance at the university greatly improved the chance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinshi">jinshi</a> degree, at about one in four against about one in sixteen for scholars who had only recently passed the prefectural examinations. In 1202 about 37,000 applicants sat the entrance test for the 200 available places.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taixue” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He made Confucianism the state orthodoxy, established the <b>Imperial Academy</b> to train officials in the classical texts, and launched expansionist campaigns that greatly enlarged the empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Zhongshu’s advice, Emperor Wu had every commandery send men judged “filial and incorrupt” (xiaolian) to the capital each year, and in 124 BCE he founded the <b>Imperial Academy</b> (Taixue) to teach the Confucian classics; it had fifty students at first and some 30,000 by the 2nd century CE.</blockquote><a href="/societies/han-china">Social Hierarchy of Han China</a></li></ul></section>
